# Copied by the Python process.
# Every setting is commented out: the defaults run every AI on this machine (assistant/ai/stages.<profile>.json).
#
# VR_PROFILE=cpu                 cpu (default) = a machine without an NVIDIA card: small models, HTTPS on the local network
#                                gpu = a rented server with a GPU: big models, CUDA builds (Windows), a Cloudflare tunnel
#
# One configuration for every AI. A "stage" is one job that needs an AI, and it is only an url, a key and a model:
#
#   assistant   chat (answers) · agent (works the menu) · vision (a picture of the screen) · code (writes plugins)
#               stt (listening to questions) · stt_wake (listening for the call word) · tts (speaking)
#   plugins     plugin_chat (what a plugin may ask: POST /api/plugin-ai/chat)
#
# By default the url is a server of this machine, started by the project (llama.cpp, whisper.cpp). To send a stage to an
# API of somebody else, set its url (an OpenAI-style base, ending in /v1) and key; nothing else changes:
#
#   VR_<STAGE>_URL=      e.g. VR_CHAT_URL=https://api.example.com/v1
#   VR_<STAGE>_KEY=
#   VR_<STAGE>_MODEL=    the name that API gives the model
#
# agent, vision and plugin_chat follow chat, so setting VR_CHAT_URL moves all four; set VR_AGENT_URL (etc.) to split one off.
# Listening needs an OpenAI-style /audio/transcriptions (VR_STT_URL); speaking with an url needs /audio/speech (VR_TTS_URL,
# VR_TTS_VOICE). Without a VR_TTS_URL the voice is VieNeu (Vietnamese) and the machine's own SAPI voice (English).
# Say "hiện thông tin model" to see what each stage is using.

# VR_CHAT_URL=
# VR_CHAT_KEY=
# VR_CHAT_MODEL=
# VR_CODE_URL=
# VR_STT_URL=
# VR_TTS_URL=

# --- the servers of this machine (assistant/ai/stages.<profile>.json, "servers") ------------------------------------------
# run.py fetches the programs into assistant/bin/ and the models into assistant/weights/ (downloads.json): about 7 GB (cpu),
# 17 GB (gpu).
# cpu: CPU builds of llama.cpp and whisper.cpp and small models. Tune it in stages.cpu.json: "threads" (the physical cores),
#   "ctx" and "image_max_tokens" (a picture costs tokens, and tokens cost seconds on a CPU).
# gpu: the 8B model that answers (and reads the screen) and the 14B that writes plugins share one 16 GB GPU, so writing a
#   plugin stops the first and starts the second, and the first comes back at the next question.
# VR_VOICE_VI_PATH=assistant/weights/vieneu   (turbo/ and codec/ inside)

# --- tools: the weather and look-ups go to Open-Meteo and Wikipedia when asked for ----
# VR_TOOLS=on                    off = answer that they are unavailable, send nothing

# --- who may use the server --------------------------------------------------------
# cpu: the key is off by default: any device on the same Wi-Fi opens the address and uses the assistant, no key. VR_ACCESS=on
#   requires it: then only the computer's own browser is exempt, and the address printed at start-up carries it as ?key=.
# gpu: run.py opens a Cloudflare tunnel and prints a public https://....trycloudflare.com address (new at every start),
#   because a rented server opens no port. The key is ON while there is a tunnel, and a request through the tunnel needs it
#   even though it comes from 127.0.0.1.
# Without VR_ACCESS_TOKEN a random key is made once and kept in assistant/data/access.key.
# VR_TUNNEL=on                   gpu only. off = no tunnel and no public address: the page is http://127.0.0.1:5173 on this machine only
# VR_ACCESS=off                  cpu default off, gpu default follows VR_TUNNEL; on or off by hand (requests from other websites are refused either way)
# VR_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# --- waits and the settings agent ---------------------------------------------------
# VR_STT_TIMEOUT_S=60            seconds one stretch of speech may take to be written down before it counts as failed
# VR_STT_MIN_LOGPROB=-0.9        a line the recogniser doubted more than this is dropped (closer to 0 drops more)
# VR_STT_MAX_NO_SPEECH=0.6       a stretch it thinks is not speech above this is dropped
# VR_AGENT=on                    off = a line that is not a keyword command is simply answered, never sent to the settings agent
# VR_AGENT_TIMEOUT_S=25          seconds the settings agent may take before the line is just answered
